abstract: 'The Mass Gap reads the Yang Mills mass gap problem through probability. Reflection positivity makes the spectral measure of a gauge field positive, and the book develops what that positivity buys: the gap as the exponential moment threshold of a generalized gamma convolution, and as the decay rate of its Thorin densities; Brownian motion on SU(2) and the exactly solvable theory in two dimensions; Monte Carlo estimation of the gap; and the Migdal Kadanoff renormalization ladder as a flow on laws of the plaquette. The new results are proved for the ladder, not for the four dimensional lattice. They include a product formula for the SU(2) heat kernel with monotone roots, whose reciprocal is the Laplace transform of a generalized gamma convolution; the contraction of the projected ladder at every coupling; a hypergeometric law for the coalescence of the latent count, with a harmonic law for its mean and a first rung theorem; the Thorin form of the Hartman Watson law; and the preservation of real zeros by convolution and the heat flow on SU(2). The book poses a conjecture, that link densities with real zeros beyond the edge of the group are closed under convolution, and reduces it to statements about one function at a time. Every computation is reported with its script, and theorems, leading order derivations, numerical results and open problems are kept apart. The mass gap in four dimensions is not proved here; the chapters on open problems say what is missing.'
